# XSH posix_spawn() and posix_spawnp()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The module's option is SPN (the Spawn option). Each test runs itself again
# as the new process image ("img" mode), which checks what it got and reports
# through a pipe (or its exit status), so that a test knows the image really
# ran. Copies of the test binary with different tag bytes tell apart the
# files found by a path search. The posix_spawn_file_actions_*() and
# posix_spawnattr_*() functions are used only to set up the call; their own
# requirements belong to other modules, and a set-up call that fails makes the
# test UNRESOLVED.
#
# A spawn error may be reported by the return value or, for an error found
# in the child after the call returned, by exit status 127 (the page's
# ERRORS and RATIONALE); every error test accepts both and also checks that
# the image did not run and that, when an error value is returned, no child
# exists. The value stored in *pid after an error is unspecified and is never
# looked at. Tests that need privilege to change IDs or scheduling say so in
# "needs root" and run those changes in a helper process.
#
# The tests that stand on the Issue 8 additions (FD_CLOFORK, SETSID, chdir
# and fchdir actions, ignoring a close of a closed descriptor, no fallback to
# sh) carry since 2024.

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.001
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       posix_spawn() of an executable file with valid arguments returns 0 and
           stores the child's process ID in *pid: a positive value, not the
           caller's own, and the process ID that the new image sees as its own.
test       t/001-returns-pid.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.002
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The new process is a child of the caller: the parent process ID that
           the new image sees is the caller's process ID.
test       t/002-child-of-caller.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.003
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       The process ID stored by posix_spawn() can be waited for with
           waitpid(): it returns that ID and a status showing normal termination
           with the exit status 42 that the new image chose, after the image has
           reported that it ran.
test       t/003-wait-status.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.004
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       With a null pid argument posix_spawn() still returns 0 and creates the
           process: the image runs, and wait() returns the process ID the image
           reported as its own, with the image's exit status 42.
test       t/004-null-pid.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.005
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The strings of posix_spawn()'s argv array, argv[0] included (here not
           the file name), an empty one, one with a space and a 2000-byte one,
           reach the new image's main() in order; argc counts them and argv[argc]
           is a null pointer.
test       t/005-argv.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.006
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The strings of posix_spawn()'s envp array ("PC_A=1", "PC_B=two words",
           "PC_EMPTY=") are the new image's whole environment, as seen through
           environ; a variable set in the caller's own environment is not there.
test       t/006-envp.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.007
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With an envp array holding only the terminating null pointer, the new
           image's environ points to an array whose first element is a null
           pointer: the environment is empty.
test       t/007-envp-empty.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.008
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A path that does not start with a slash, with no file actions, names
           the file relative to the caller's working directory: "d/prog" runs the
           copy of the image in d.
test       t/008-path-relative.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.009
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A relative path is resolved against the child's working directory
           after all the file actions: with a chdir action to "w", the path
           "prog" runs w/prog, not the prog in the caller's directory, and the
           new image's working directory is w.
test       t/009-path-after-chdir.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.010
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       posix_spawnp() with a file argument containing a slash uses it as the
           pathname without searching PATH: "bin/prog" runs ./bin/prog, not the
           bin/prog found under a directory named by PATH.
note       The caller's PATH and the PATH in envp are the same here, because the
           page does not say which one posix_spawnp() searches (standard question
           Q-91).
test       t/010-spawnp-slash.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.011
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       posix_spawnp() with a file argument without a slash searches the
           directories of PATH: with PATH naming a missing directory, then a
           directory without the file, then one holding it, the file there runs.
note       The caller's PATH and the PATH in envp are the same here, because the
           page does not say which one posix_spawnp() searches (standard question
           Q-91).
test       t/011-spawnp-search.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.012
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       posix_spawnp() takes the first match in PATH order: with PATH naming
           b1 then b2, each holding a different prog, the one in b1 runs.
note       The caller's PATH and the PATH in envp are the same here, because the
           page does not say which one posix_spawnp() searches (standard question
           Q-91).
test       t/012-spawnp-order.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.013
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A relative directory in PATH is taken in the child's working directory
           after all the file actions: with PATH "rel" and a chdir action to "w",
           posix_spawnp("prog") runs w/rel/prog, not the ./rel/prog of the
           caller's directory.
note       The caller's PATH and the PATH in envp are the same here, because the
           page does not say which one posix_spawnp() searches (standard question
           Q-91).
test       t/013-spawnp-relative-dir.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.014
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       posix_spawnp() of an executable file that is not in any executable
           format (a text file of shell commands) does not fall back to running
           it with sh: the commands do not run (a marker file is not made), and
           the call fails, by returning ENOEXEC or with the child ending with
           status 127.
note       The caller's PATH and the PATH in envp are the same here, because the
           page does not say which one posix_spawnp() searches (standard question
           Q-91).
test       t/014-spawnp-no-sh-fallback.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.015
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With a null file_actions pointer a descriptor without FD_CLOEXEC stays
           open in the child with the same open file description: same access
           mode, O_APPEND still set, the same offset (3), FD_CLOEXEC clear; a
           write by the child moves the offset the caller sees.
test       t/015-fd-kept.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.016
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With a null file_actions pointer a descriptor with FD_CLOEXEC set is
           closed in the child, while a descriptor without it stays open.
test       t/016-fd-cloexec-closed.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.017
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       With a null file_actions pointer a descriptor with FD_CLOFORK set is
           closed in the child, while a descriptor without it stays open.
test       t/017-fd-clofork-closed.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.018
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       With a file_actions object that holds no actions, the child has the
           caller's descriptors except those with FD_CLOEXEC or FD_CLOFORK set: a
           plain one is open, the other two are closed.
test       t/018-fd-empty-actions.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.019
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With a null file_actions pointer the child does not inherit the
           caller's process-owned record lock: the child, using the inherited
           descriptor, finds the lock held by the caller (F_GETLK reports it,
           with the caller's process ID).
note       Only process-owned locks are tested; open-file-description locks
           belong to the shared description, and the page's wording for them is
           unclear (standard question Q-92).
test       t/019-lock-not-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.020
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With a non-null file_actions object holding no actions the child does
           not inherit the caller's process-owned record lock either (F_GETLK in
           the child reports it held by the caller).
note       Only process-owned locks are tested (standard question Q-92).
test       t/020-lock-not-inherited-actions.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.021
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A close action closes the descriptor in the child only: the child has
           the named descriptor closed and another open, and the caller's
           descriptor is still open afterwards.
test       t/021-addclose.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.022
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/posix_spawn_file_actions_addclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A close action naming a descriptor that is in range but not open is
           not an error: posix_spawn() returns 0 and the image runs with that
           descriptor closed.
test       t/022-addclose-closed.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.023
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       An open action opens the named file on the given descriptor number in
           the child (readable, with FD_CLOEXEC clear), while the caller does not
           gain that descriptor.
test       t/023-addopen.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.024
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/posix_spawn_file_actions_addclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       An open action on a descriptor number that is already open closes that
           descriptor first: the child reads the newly opened file's contents
           from it, and the caller's descriptor still reads its own file.
test       t/024-addopen-replaces.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.025
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       An open action with O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_EXCL and mode 0640 creates the
           file in the child's context: with the caller's umask 0 (inherited),
           the new file has permission bits 0640, and the child's write through
           the descriptor reaches it.
test       t/025-addopen-creat.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.026
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       dup2 actions to descriptors 0 and 1 redirect the child's standard
           input and output: the child reads "abc" sent by the caller from
           descriptor 0 and writes it back with "!" to descriptor 1.
test       t/026-adddup2-stdio.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.027
anchor     functions/posix_spawn_file_actions_adddup2.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A dup2 action whose two descriptors are the same, on a descriptor with
           FD_CLOEXEC set, leaves it open in the child with FD_CLOEXEC clear.
test       t/027-adddup2-same.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.028
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       FD_CLOEXEC is applied after the file actions: a dup2 action from a
           descriptor with FD_CLOEXEC set to another number leaves the new number
           open (the copy has FD_CLOEXEC clear, reading the same file) and the
           original closed in the child.
test       t/028-adddup2-cloexec-source.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.029
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       File actions are performed in the order they were added: open A on N,
           dup2 N to M, open B on N leaves M reading A and N reading B.
test       t/029-actions-in-order.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.030
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/posix_spawn_file_actions_addclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A file actions object is performed in the child only and can be used
           again: two spawns with the same chdir-and-open actions give the same
           result in each child, and the caller's working directory and
           descriptors are unchanged after each.
test       t/030-actions-reusable.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.031
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With null file_actions and attrp pointers the child's working
           directory is the caller's.
test       t/031-cwd-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.032
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       Chdir actions "a" then "b" leave the child in a/b (the second relative
           path is taken in the directory the first set), and the caller's
           working directory is unchanged.
test       t/032-addchdir-sequence.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.033
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/posix_spawn_file_actions_addchdir.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       An fchdir action puts the child in the directory open on the
           descriptor even when that descriptor has FD_CLOEXEC set (which is
           applied after the actions, so the descriptor is closed in the image);
           a chdir action after it is taken relative to that directory.
test       t/033-addfchdir.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.034
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/posix_spawn_file_actions_addclose.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       A relative path in an open action is interpreted in the working
           directory determined by the earlier actions: after a chdir action to
           "d", opening "f" gives d/f (content "inner"), not the caller's ./f.
test       t/034-addopen-tracked-cwd.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.035
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_SETSIGMASK the child starts with exactly the signal
           mask of the attributes object (SIGUSR2 and SIGTERM blocked), not the
           caller's (SIGUSR1 and SIGHUP blocked).
test       t/035-sigmask-set.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.036
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Without POSIX_SPAWN_SETSIGMASK the child has the caller's signal mask
           (SIGUSR1 and SIGUSR2 blocked), both with a null attrp and with an
           attributes object whose sigmask attribute is set (to SIGTERM) but
           whose flags are clear.
test       t/036-sigmask-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.037
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_SETSIGDEF the signals in the sigdefault attribute
           (SIGUSR1 and SIGQUIT, both ignored by the caller) get the default
           action in the child, while an ignored signal outside the set (SIGUSR2)
           stays ignored.
test       t/037-sigdefault-set.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.038
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Without POSIX_SPAWN_SETSIGDEF a signal ignored by the caller (SIGUSR1)
           stays ignored in the child, both with a null attrp and with an
           attributes object that lists it in sigdefault but has the flags clear.
test       t/038-sigdefault-flag-clear.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.039
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With a null attrp the child has the default action for signals the
           caller left at the default (SIGHUP) and for signals the caller catches
           (SIGUSR2), and ignores signals the caller ignores (SIGUSR1).
test       t/039-signal-dispositions.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.040
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       When the caller ignores SIGCHLD and the sigdefault attribute lists it
           with POSIX_SPAWN_SETSIGDEF set, SIGCHLD has the default action in the
           child.
note       The child of an ignoring caller may be reaped automatically, so the
           wait accepts ECHILD.
test       t/040-sigchld-default.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.041
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_SETPGROUP and a pgroup attribute of 0 the child is in
           a new process group whose ID is its own process ID, different from the
           caller's group.
test       t/041-setpgroup-new.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.042
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_SETPGROUP and a non-zero pgroup attribute naming an
           existing process group of the caller's session, the child joins that
           group.
note       The group is a helper process's own, made with setpgid() and kept
           alive until the check ends.
test       t/042-setpgroup-join.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.043
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_SETSID the child is the leader of a new session and
           of a new process group, both with its own process ID as ID, different
           from the caller's session and group.
note       That the child has no controlling terminal is not tested: the pathname
           for the controlling terminal does not say what opening it does when
           there is none.
test       t/043-setsid.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.044
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Without POSIX_SPAWN_SETPGROUP and POSIX_SPAWN_SETSID the child stays
           in the caller's process group and session, both with a null attrp and
           with an attributes object whose flags are clear (although a pgroup
           attribute is set).
test       t/044-group-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.045
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_RESETIDS the child's effective user ID is the
           caller's real user ID: a caller with real user ID 0 and effective user
           ID 65534 gets a child with effective user ID 0.
note       Run by a privileged process whose helper process changes its own IDs;
           the image file is made readable and executable for others.
needs      root
test       t/045-resetids-user.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.046
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_RESETIDS the child's effective group ID is the
           caller's real group ID: a caller with real group ID 0 and effective
           group ID 65534 gets a child with effective group ID 0.
note       Run by a privileged process whose helper process changes its own IDs;
           the image file is made readable and executable for others.
needs      root
test       t/046-resetids-group.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.047
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Without POSIX_SPAWN_RESETIDS the child inherits the caller's effective
           user ID: a caller with real user ID 0 and effective user ID 65534 gets
           a child with real user ID 0 and effective user ID 65534.
note       Run by a privileged process whose helper process changes its own IDs;
           the image file is made readable and executable for others.
needs      root
test       t/047-ids-user-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.048
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Without POSIX_SPAWN_RESETIDS the child inherits the caller's effective
           group ID: a caller with real group ID 0 and effective group ID 65534
           gets a child with real group ID 0 and effective group ID 65534.
note       Run by a privileged process whose helper process changes its own IDs;
           the image file is made readable and executable for others.
needs      root
test       t/048-ids-group-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.049
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       When the image file has the set-user-ID bit and another owner (65534),
           the child's effective user ID is that owner, whether or not
           POSIX_SPAWN_RESETIDS is set, and its real user ID stays 0.
note       Unresolved where the file system ignores set-ID bits (ST_NOSUID).
needs      root
test       t/049-setuid-image.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.050
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       When the image file has the set-group-ID bit and another group
           (65534), the child's effective group ID is that group, whether or not
           POSIX_SPAWN_RESETIDS is set, and its real group ID stays 0.
note       Unresolved where the file system ignores set-ID bits (ST_NOSUID).
needs      root
test       t/050-setgid-image.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.051
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       SPN+PS
since      2001
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_SETSCHEDULER the child has the scheduling policy and
           parameters of the attributes object: SCHED_RR at its minimum priority.
note       Unresolved if the caller may not set this policy (the call returns
           EPERM or the child ends with status 127).
needs      root
test       t/051-setscheduler.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.052
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       SPN+PS
since      2001
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_SETSCHEDPARAM alone the child keeps the caller's
           scheduling policy (SCHED_RR) and gets the parameters of the attributes
           object (priority one above the minimum).
note       Unresolved if the caller cannot be put in SCHED_RR or the policy has
           one priority only.
needs      root
test       t/052-setschedparam.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.053
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
class      conditional
when       SPN+PS
since      2001
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_SETSCHEDULER and a policy that does not exist, the
           call fails as sched_setscheduler() does (EINVAL, or EPERM when the
           caller lacks privilege), or returns 0 and the child ends with status
           127; the image does not run and no child is left when an error is
           returned.
note       The attribute setters may refuse the value (EINVAL); then the test is
           unresolved.
test       t/053-setscheduler-invalid.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.054
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A pending alarm of the caller (the test's own, set to 40 seconds) is
           not inherited: in the child alarm(0) reports nothing pending.
test       t/054-alarm-not-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.055
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       Signals pending for the caller are not pending for the child: with
           SIGUSR1 blocked and pending in the caller, the child (which inherits
           the mask) has nothing pending.
test       t/055-pending-not-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.056
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The child has the caller's file mode creation mask (here 027).
test       t/056-umask-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.057
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       The child has the caller's resource limits: a soft RLIMIT_CORE of 7777
           set by the caller is the child's soft limit.
note       Unresolved when the hard limit is below 7777.
test       t/057-rlimit-inherited.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.058
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/exec.html#ERRORS/ENOENT
class      required
since      2001
says       posix_spawn() of a path that does not exist, and of an empty path,
           fails as exec does, with ENOENT returned (or returns 0 with the child
           ending with status 127); the image does not run, and when an error is
           returned no child exists.
test       t/058-err-enoent.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.059
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/exec.html#ERRORS/ENOENT
class      required
since      2001
says       posix_spawnp() of a name found in no directory of PATH fails with
           ENOENT returned (or returns 0 with the child ending with status 127).
test       t/059-err-spawnp-enoent.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.060
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/exec.html#ERRORS/EACCES
class      required
since      2001
says       posix_spawn() of a regular file with mode 0644 (no execute permission
           for anyone, so even a privileged process may not run it) fails with
           EACCES returned (or returns 0 with the child ending with status 127).
test       t/060-err-eacces.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.061
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/exec.html#ERRORS/ENOTDIR
class      required
since      2008
says       posix_spawn() of "prog/", where prog is an executable regular file,
           fails with ENOTDIR returned (or returns 0 with the child ending with
           status 127).
test       t/061-err-enotdir.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.062
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/exec.html#ERRORS/ELOOP
class      required
since      2001
says       posix_spawn() of a symbolic link that is part of a loop (a -> b, b ->
           a) fails with ELOOP returned (or returns 0 with the child ending with
           status 127).
test       t/062-err-eloop.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.063
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/exec.html#ERRORS/ENAMETOOLONG
class      required
since      2001
says       posix_spawn() of a path whose last component is {NAME_MAX}+1 bytes
           long fails with ENAMETOOLONG returned, or with ENOENT, which also
           applies because no such file can exist (or returns 0 with the child
           ending with status 127).
note       NOTINUSE where names are truncated instead (_PC_NO_TRUNC is 0) or
           there is no limit.
test       t/063-err-enametoolong.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.064
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/exec.html#ERRORS/ENOEXEC
class      required
since      2001
says       posix_spawn() of a file with execute permission whose contents are in
           no recognized format (64 bytes of 0x01) fails with ENOEXEC returned
           (or returns 0 with the child ending with status 127); posix_spawn()
           does not hand the file to sh.
note       A rule of the environment that interprets the file (a registered
           format handler) would be the environment, not posix_spawn() (as for
           exec).
test       t/064-err-enoexec.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.065
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
anchor     functions/exec.html#ERRORS/E2BIG
class      disputed
since      2001
says       posix_spawn() with arguments totalling more than {ARG_MAX} bytes (as
           sysconf() reports it) fails with E2BIG returned (or returns 0 with the
           child ending with status 127).
note       disputed (Q-9): which {ARG_MAX} applies is open (as for exec). ENOMEM
           is also accepted when the arguments exceed the stack limit (XSH 2.3).
           Strings of 4096 bytes are used. An indeterminate {ARG_MAX} makes the
           test NOTINUSE, one over 64 MiB UNRESOLVED.
test       t/065-err-e2big.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.066
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2024
says       When an open action fails (the file does not exist) after an earlier
           chdir action succeeded, posix_spawn() returns ENOENT (or returns 0
           with the child ending with status 127), the image does not run, and
           the caller's working directory is unchanged.
test       t/066-err-addopen.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.067
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2024
says       A chdir action to a directory that does not exist makes posix_spawn()
           return ENOENT (or return 0 with the child ending with status 127), and
           the image does not run.
test       t/067-err-addchdir.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.068
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       A dup2 action whose source descriptor is not open makes posix_spawn()
           return EBADF (or return 0 with the child ending with status 127), and
           the image does not run.
test       t/068-err-adddup2.c

assertion  XSH.posix_spawn.069
anchor     functions/posix_spawn.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       With POSIX_SPAWN_SETPGROUP and a pgroup that is the ID of no process
           group in the caller's session, posix_spawn() fails as setpgid() does
           (EPERM), returning it (or returns 0 with the child ending with status
           127); the image does not run.
note       The group ID is chosen from values near the caller's process ID that
           name no process; the attribute setter may refuse it, then unresolved.
test       t/069-err-setpgroup.c
